PRSENSE(1)
PRSENSE — principled AI-assisted pull request review

⸻

NAME
prsense — CLI-first, self-hosted AI code reviewer

⸻

SYNOPSIS
prsense [command] [options] [target]

⸻

DESCRIPTION
PRsense is a command-line tool for reviewing code changes using large
language models with full repository context.

It combines:
  • structured rule-based review signals
  • retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
  • explicit configuration
  • self-hosted infrastructure

PRsense operates primarily on diffs and surrounding repository context,
producing structured review signals that prioritize clarity, correctness,
and maintainability.

PRsense may optionally run a background daemon for long-lived or
service-oriented workloads, but the CLI operates independently by default.

⸻

COMMANDS

review      Review a code change and emit structured review signals
index       Index a repository for contextual retrieval (RAG)
doctor      Diagnose PRsense installation and runtime environment
setup       Perform optional one-time setup checks
daemon      Manage the PRsense background daemon
help        Show help for a command

⸻

COMMAND: review

NAME
prsense review — review a code change

SYNOPSIS
prsense review [target] [options]

DESCRIPTION
Reviews a code change and produces structured review signals.

The review process:

  1. Computes a diff
  2. Retrieves relevant surrounding repository context (if indexed)
  3. Constructs a structured review prompt
  4. Invokes the configured LLM provider
  5. Validates and normalizes output
  6. Emits structured ReviewSignal objects

Signals are filtered by confidence threshold and deduplicated before output.

TARGETS

.                       Current repository (default)
<path>                  Path to a local repository

If no diff target is provided, PRsense runs `git diff` in the current
repository.

OPTIONS

--base-branch <branch>
Override the base branch used for diff comparison.

--format <text|json>
Output format. Default is text.

--no-context
Disable contextual retrieval and perform diff-only review.

NOTES

Contextual retrieval requires that the repository has been indexed via
`prsense index`.

If no index exists, review falls back to diff-only mode.

EXAMPLES

prsense review .
prsense review --base-branch origin/main
prsense review . --format json

⸻

COMMAND: index

NAME
prsense index — index repository for contextual review

SYNOPSIS
prsense index [path] [options]

DESCRIPTION
Indexes a repository for retrieval-augmented review (RAG).

Indexing:

  • Scans repository files
  • Chunks content deterministically
  • Generates embeddings
  • Stores vectors in PostgreSQL with pgvector
  • Tracks repository fingerprint metadata

The process is idempotent and safe to run multiple times.

Re-indexing occurs automatically when:
  • commit SHA changes
  • embedding provider changes
  • embedding model changes
  • chunking strategy changes
  • PRsense version changes

TARGETS

.                       Current repository (default)
<path>                  Path to a local repository

OPTIONS

--force
Rebuild the index even if it appears up-to-date.

--dry-run
Evaluate index state without writing to database.

--stats
Print indexing statistics after completion.

--chunk-size <n>
Override chunk size (lines per chunk).

NOTES

Indexing requires:

  • PostgreSQL
  • pgvector extension
  • Valid embedding provider configuration

Embedding dimensions must match the vector schema.

EXAMPLES

prsense index .
prsense index ../repo --force
prsense index . --stats

⸻

COMMAND: doctor

NAME
prsense doctor — diagnose environment and configuration

SYNOPSIS
prsense doctor

DESCRIPTION
Performs structured diagnostic checks to validate:

  • Environment configuration
  • User configuration
  • Database connectivity
  • pgvector extension
  • Docker availability (if applicable)
  • LLM provider availability
  • Repository state

Each check emits structured results with actionable guidance.

EXAMPLES

prsense doctor

⸻

COMMAND: setup

NAME
prsense setup — perform optional setup guidance

SYNOPSIS
prsense setup

DESCRIPTION
Provides first-time setup validation and instructions.

Unlike `doctor`, this command focuses on readiness rather than ongoing
diagnostics.

It does not automatically provision services.

EXAMPLES

prsense setup

⸻

COMMAND: daemon

NAME
prsense daemon — manage the PRsense background daemon

SYNOPSIS
prsense daemon [subcommand]

DESCRIPTION
Manages an optional long-lived daemon process for:

  • Continuous indexing
  • CI-driven reviews
  • Future GitHub App integrations
  • Automated workflows

The CLI does not depend on the daemon.

SUBCOMMANDS

prsense daemon start [--foreground]
Start the daemon process.

prsense daemon stop
Stop a running daemon.

prsense daemon status
Show daemon status.

NOTES

PRsense does not auto-start background services.
Lifecycle is explicit and signal-based.

⸻

CONFIGURATION

PRsense uses:

  1. User configuration (review behavior)
  2. Environment configuration (runtime + credentials)

USER CONFIGURATION

A file named `prsense.yml` may be placed at repository root.

Example:

llm:
  provider: ollama
  model: qwen2.5-coder
  temperature: 0.1

embeddings:
  provider: ollama
  model: nomic-embed-text

review:
  confidenceThreshold: 0.6
  maxSignals: 10

context:
  maxChunks: 5

git:
  baseBranch: trunk

If absent, defaults are applied.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

Environment variables configure infrastructure and credentials.

Common variables:

PRSENSE_DATABASE_URL
OPENAI_API_KEY
GITHUB_TOKEN
GITLAB_TOKEN
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN
PRSENSE_LOG_LEVEL

Environment variables are validated before workflow execution.

⸻

EXIT STATUS

0   Successful execution
1   Invalid usage or configuration
2   Runtime or dependency failure

⸻

FILES

prsense.yml
Repository-local configuration.

~/.config/prsense/config.yml
Optional user-level configuration.

~/.local/state/prsense/
Runtime state directory (daemon PID, logs).

⸻

DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

PRsense is intentionally:

  • CLI-first
  • Self-hosted
  • Retrieval-augmented
  • Deterministic
  • Transparent
  • Explicit in lifecycle management
  • Structured in output
  • Opinionated but configurable

It prioritizes signal quality over verbosity.

